"Trust but Verify: Designing Region-InhomogeneousForward Dynamics for Discrete Diffusion (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22380v1)\nciteturn41255view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22380v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 288\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related work L18:     1. cite8†Discrete diffusion as stochastic dynamics. L19:     2. cite9†Hybrid and self-correcting forward processes. L20:     3. cite10†Denoising and editing text. L21:     4. cite11†Conditioning in continuous diffusion. L22:   4. cite12†3 Method L23:     1. cite13†3.1 Background: absorbing forward dynamics L24:     2. cite14†3.2 Region-inhomogeneous forward dynamics L25:     3. cite15†3.3 Objective L26:     4. cite16†3.4 Inference: reverse-time re-noising of the conditioning L27:   5. cite17†4 Experiments L28:     1. cite18†4.1 Setup L29:       1. cite19†Model and training. L30:       2. cite20†Evaluation protocol. L31:       3. cite21†Arms. L32:       4. cite22†Metrics. L33:     2. cite23†4.2 Results L34:       1. cite24†Repair (H1). L35:       2. cite25†Containment (H2). L36:       3. cite26†Aggregate infill quality. L37:       4. cite27†Passage quality under the judge. L38:       5. cite28†Non-regression of the learned reverse dynamics (H3). L39:       6. cite29†Annealing (H4). L40:       7. cite30†External-detector baseline. L41:       8. cite31†The detectability–damage regularity. L42:   6. cite32†5 Discussion and limitations L43:   7. cite33†References L44:   8. cite34†A Full token-F1 grid L45: cite35†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L46: \nL47: arXiv:2610.22380v1 [cs.LG] 02 Oct 2026\nL48: \nL49: \\workshoptitle\nL50: \nL51: AI for Stochastic Dynamics\nL52: # Trust but Verify: Designing Region-Inhomogeneous\nL53: Forward Dynamics for Discrete Diffusion\nL54: \nL55: Gunmay Jhingran Affiliation: Delhi Technological University Email: gunmayjhingran_23ep040@dtu.ac.in\nL56: ###### Abstract\nL57: In continuous diffusion, the forward process is a design surface: noise schedules, SDE variants, and per-position noise levels are all engineered to match the task. Discrete diffusion for language has collapsed this surface to a single point, the absorbing process, under which conditioning context sits at infinite signal-to-noise ratio for the entire reverse trajectory. The collapse has a cost.\nL58: Trust in the conditioning becomes binary, so corrupted context tokens are copied as ground truth and their errors propagate into the generated spans.\nL59: We treat the discrete forward process as a design space again, composing absorbing and uniform-mixing corruption kernels with region-dependent rates: hole positions traverse the standard absorbing dynamics, while user-supplied context is corrupted by light random substitution at a small rate $\\eta$, so that “observed but uncertain” is a state the model has visited during training.\nL60: At inference, a heuristic reverse-time sampler re-injects noise into suspicious context tokens under an annealed intensity (a corrector-like step that acts on the conditioning rather than the sample) and freezes the context dynamics late in the trajectory. An 8,000-step finetune of a 170M-parameter pretrained masked diffusion model, under five hours on a 6 GB laptop GPU, converts a blind copier into a verifier.\nL61: The base model assigns roughly 50% probability to observed tokens even when they are uniform-random garbage; on conspicuous (uniform-random) corruptions, the finetuned model detects corrupted context with 0.96 recall at 0.91 precision, improves infill token-F1 by 3.8 points at 10% context corruption (6.4 at 20%), and eliminates the accuracy dip in tokens generated adjacent to corruptions, with no measurable regression on clean conditioning (false-edit rate under 0.4% on clean context).\nL62: Gains under plausible corruptions are much weaker (about 0.5 F1 points at 10% corruption, not statistically significant). The experiments also suggest a regularity: detectability and damage are jointly governed by how far the corruption kernel departs from the data conditional. Noise near the conditional is both statistically invisible and semantically light; noise far from it is conspicuous and destructive.\nL63: We interpret absorbing masked diffusion as the infinite-contrast limit of a trust axis whose continuous analog is the SNR gap between known and unknown regions in diffusion inpainting; in our setting (one 170M model, one text domain), points inside that axis can be reached by perturbing the forward dynamics rather than the architecture.\nL64: ## 1 Introduction\nL65: Continuous diffusion treats its forward stochastic dynamics as an engineering surface.\nL66: The choice of SDE and noise schedule (cite36†Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015 ; cite37†Ho et al., 2020 ; cite38†Song et al., 2021 ), the renoising of observed regions in inpainting (cite39†Lugmayr et al., 2022 ), and per-position noise levels as first-class objects (cite40†Chen et al., 2024 ) are all instances of the same principle: the corruption process is designed to match the task, and the reverse-time dynamics (cite41†Anderson, 1982 ) inherit its structure. Discrete diffusion began with the same breadth: cite42†Austin et al.\nL67: (2021) studied whole families of corruption kernels, and continuous-time formulations characterize the general Markov-process setting (cite43†Campbell et al., 2022 ; cite44†Lou et al., 2024 ). In practice, though, the field has collapsed onto a single point in that space, the absorbing process, in which each token independently jumps to [MASK] and stays there. The reasons are good ones.\nL68: The absorbing kernel admits a simple weighted cross-entropy bound (cite45†Sahoo et al., 2024 ; cite46†Shi et al., 2024 ), its optimal denoiser is time-free, modeling the conditionals of clean data directly (cite47†Ou et al., 2025 ), and any partially observed sequence is in-distribution at every noise level, so conditioning requires no mechanism at all. The recipe scales to LLaDA-class models competitive with strong autoregressive baselines (cite48†Nie et al., 2025 ).\nL69: The collapsed design space has a structural cost that is easiest to see in the language of noise. Under absorbing dynamics, an observed token has, in effect, infinite signal-to-noise ratio: the reverse process carries it with probability one, forever. Trust in the conditioning is therefore binary: a position is either fully certain or fully unknown, and there is no state expressing that context is mostly right. Three consequences follow.\nL70: When the conditioning text contains errors (a corrupted retrieval, a buggy code file, a noisy transcription), the model conditions on wrong tokens as if they were ground truth and the errors contaminate the spans it generates; we find the pretrained model assigns roughly 50% probability to observed context tokens even when they are uniform-random garbage. The model has no mechanism to repair its context, because the reverse dynamics never revisit unmasked states.\nL71: And graded uncertainty about the condition, which continuous inpainting can express through the elapsed noise of the known region, has no discrete counterpart.\nL72: We propose to reopen the design space with the minimal move: compose two corruption kernels with region-dependent rates.\nL73: Hole positions traverse the standard absorbing dynamics indexed by $t$; user-supplied context is corrupted by the uniform-mixing kernel at a small per-sequence rate $\\eta$; equivalently, the context region is held at a fixed, small elapsed time under uniform-mixing dynamics while holes traverse the absorbing process (as in standard masked diffusion, only the forward marginals enter the objective).\nL74: A short finetune of a pretrained masked diffusion model under this region-inhomogeneous forward process, with an $\\eta{=}0$ mixture component protecting the clean-conditioning regime, yields a model that emits a meaningful predictive distribution at every position.\nL75: That distribution supplies a free suspicion score for context, the model’s own probability of the observed token, which our sampler exploits through a corrector-like heuristic acting on the conditioning: early in the reverse trajectory, context tokens the model finds implausible are re-noised into the absorbing state under an annealed intensity and refilled by the ordinary reverse dynamics with full bidirectional conditioning; past a lock time, the context dynamics freeze.\nL76: We make three contributions. We formalize region-inhomogeneous forward processes for masked diffusion and show they require only a light perturbation of the standard SFT-style objective (Section cite12†3 ). We introduce the detect–remask–refill sampler with trust annealing, a heuristic re-injection of noise into the conditioning during the reverse process, with the confidence bookkeeping needed to compose with low-confidence decoding (Section cite16†3.4 ).\nL77: And we evaluate on corrupted-context infilling over web text with pre-registered hypotheses and decision gates, disentangling training-time from inference-time contributions across nine arms (Section cite17†4 ). The evidence is strongest for conspicuous (uniform) corruptions, where the finetune yields detection, repair, and containment of context corruption with no measurable regression under clean conditioning.\nL78: Under plausible corruptions detection is partial, infill gains are small, and the pre-registered primary F1 gate fails. Our ablations also do not fully separate the contributions of the $\\eta{=}0$ mixture and of trust annealing from other factors that change alongside them (Section cite23†4.2 ).\nL79: The experiments further suggest a regularity, which we offer as an interpretation rather than a tested result: detectability and damage both appear to be governed by the divergence between the corruption kernel and the data conditional. Conceptually, we view absorbing masked diffusion as one end of a trust axis whose continuous analog is the signal-to-noise contrast between known and unknown regions in diffusion inpainting; this framing motivates the method but is not established formally here.\nL80: ## 2 Related work\nL81: #### Discrete diffusion as stochastic dynamics.\nL82: cite42†Austin et al. (2021) introduced discrete diffusion with structured corruption kernels (absorbing, uniform-mixing, and discretized-Gaussian transition matrices), and continuous-time treatments formalize the general setting as Markov jump processes with learnable reverse rates (cite43†Campbell et al., 2022 ; cite44†Lou et al., 2024 ).\nL83: Subsequent work specialized to the absorbing kernel, simplifying its variational bound to a weighted masked cross-entropy (cite45†Sahoo et al., 2024 ; cite46†Shi et al., 2024 ) and establishing the time-free property of its optimal denoiser (cite47†Ou et al., 2025 ); LLaDA scaled this recipe to 8B parameters (cite48†Nie et al., 2025 ), and block diffusion interpolates between autoregressive and diffusion decoding with clean context to the left of the active block (cite49†Arriola et al., 2025 ).\nL84: All of these inherit the absorbing process’s binary conditioning interface, which is the object of our study.\nL85: #### Hybrid and self-correcting forward processes.\nL86: Closest to our forward-process design, GIDD generalizes masked diffusion to arbitrary interpolating noise and trains on a spatially homogeneous hybrid of masking and uniform noise, showing the resulting models can identify and correct their own mistakes in generated samples (cite50†von Rütte et al., 2025 ); SCDD reformulates such pretraining-based self-correction with explicit discrete-time transitions (cite51†Wang et al., 2026 ); and ReMDM adds inference-time re-noising of model-committed tokens to restore iterative refinement (cite52†Wang et al., 2025 ).\nL87: Our setting differs on two axes. The corruption is region-inhomogeneous: hybrid noise is applied only to user-supplied context, targeting trust in the conditioning rather than the model’s own outputs.\nL88: And the asymmetry matters for reliability: GIDD reports that homogeneous hybrid-noise models lag mask-only models downstream, whereas confining the uniform-mixing kernel to the context region, together with an $\\eta{=}0$ mixture, kept clean-conditioning behavior and the held-out variational bound within our pre-registered H3 tolerances at this scale; we did not train a homogeneous hybrid-noise arm, so this contrast is indirect.\nL89: Our arm B further suggests that, in our setting, inference-time re-noising alone does not create the capability: a model trained only under absorbing dynamics assigns no meaningful likelihood to observed positions.\nL90: #### Denoising and editing text.\nL91: SUNDAE trains non-autoregressive generators by unrolled denoising of randomly corrupted sequences (cite53†Savinov et al., 2022 ); DiffusER frames generation as edit sequences (cite54†Reid et al., 2023 ); ELECTRA trains a discriminative replaced-token detector (cite55†Clark et al., 2020 ). We retain the diffusion formulation: detection falls out of the generative model’s own likelihoods rather than a separate discriminator, and repair is performed by the same reverse dynamics that fill holes.\nL92: Our external-detector baseline (Section cite30†4.2 ) gives one point of comparison: a GPT-2 left-context surprisal detector trails the model on conspicuous corruptions and fails on plausible ones. We did not evaluate bidirectional or trained replaced-token detectors such as ELECTRA.\nL93: #### Conditioning in continuous diffusion.\nL94: Gaussian inpainting must reconcile clean observations with reverse-time dynamics that expect globally noised inputs, via renoised composition (cite39†Lugmayr et al., 2022 ) or specialized conditional models; confidence-ordered parallel decoding originates with MaskGIT (cite56†Chang et al., 2022 ); and per-position noise levels as first-class objects appear in Diffusion Forcing (cite40†Chen et al., 2024 ).\nL95: We view our method as a discrete analog on a shared axis: where those approaches lower the known region from full noise to intermediate SNR, we raise it from infinite SNR to finite.\nL96: ## 3 Method\nL97: ### 3.1 Background: absorbing forward dynamics\nL98: Let $x_{0}\\in\\mathcal{V}^{L}$ and let $t\\in[0,1]$ index the absorbing forward process: each token independently jumps to the absorbing state [MASK] so that the forward marginal masks position $i$ with probability $t$. As in standard masked diffusion training, only these marginals enter the objective. The denoiser $p_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid\\tilde{x})$ is a bidirectional Transformer trained with the weighted masked cross-entropy of cite46†Shi et al. (2024) ; cite45†Sahoo et al.\nL99: (2024) , which upper-bounds negative log-likelihood; by the time-free property (cite47†Ou et al., 2025 ), its optimum is $p_{\\mathrm{data}}(x_{0}^{i}\\mid\\tilde{x}^{\\mathrm{UM}})$, the conditional of clean data given the unmasked set, independent of $t$. Conditional infilling fixes a set of positions as clean context and runs the reverse process over the remainder; under absorbing dynamics, observed tokens persist with probability one, and that property, its cost and its repair, is our subject.\nL100: ### 3.2 Region-inhomogeneous forward dynamics\nL101: \nL102: We partition each length-$L{=}512$ sequence into holes $H$ and known context $K$: one to four contiguous spans covering 10–50% of positions form $H$, matching the infilling use case; the rest is $K$. The two regions evolve under different corruption kernels. Draw $t\\sim U(0,1]$ and\nL103: \nL104:  | $$\\eta\\sim\\tfrac{1}{2}\\,\\delta_{0}+\\tfrac{1}{2}\\,U(0,\\eta_{\\max}],\\qquad\\eta_{\\max}=0.15,$$  |  | (1)\nL105: \nL106: and corrupt $x_{0}\\mapsto\\tilde{x}$ position-wise:\nL107:  | $$\\tilde{x}^{i}=\\begin{cases}\\texttt{[MASK]}{}\\text{ w.p. }t,\\;\\;x_{0}^{i}\\text{ otherwise},&i\\in H\\quad\\text{(absorbing kernel)},\\\\[2.0pt]\nL108: z\\sim q_{\\mathrm{noise}}\\text{ w.p. }\\eta,\\;\\;x_{0}^{i}\\text{ otherwise},&i\\in K\\quad\\text{(mixing kernel, }z\\neq x_{0}^{i}\\text{)}.\\end{cases}$$  |  | (2)\nL109: Holes traverse the absorbing dynamics indexed by $t$; the context region is held at a fixed, small elapsed time under a mixing process, its marginal substitution probability $\\eta$ resampled per sequence. The $\\eta{=}0$ component keeps the standard clean-conditioning configuration in-distribution, and 10% of training batches use the plain pretraining objective (no $K/H$ split) to protect unconditional generation.\nL110: We instantiate the mixing kernel $q_{\\mathrm{noise}}$ two ways: (i) uniform over the vocabulary, the classical uniform-mixing kernel of cite42†Austin et al. (2021) ; and (ii) a hard-negative mixture of equal parts uniform draws, corpus-unigram draws, top-32 embedding-space neighbors of the true token, and masked self-proposals from the base model in the spirit of cite55†Clark et al. (2020) . The latter is a data-dependent kernel concentrated nearer the data conditional.\nL111: Evaluation-time plausible corruptions always come from a generator family disjoint from all training noise (Section cite18†4.1 ), so no arm ever sees its test corruption distribution during training.\nL112: ### 3.3 Objective\nL113: \nL114:  | $$\\mathcal{L}(\\theta)\\;=\\;\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[\\;\\frac{1}{t}\\sum_{i\\in H}\\mathbf{1}\\!\\left[\\tilde{x}^{i}=\\text{M}\\right]\\mathrm{CE}_{i}\\;+\\;\\lambda\\sum_{i\\in K}\\mathrm{CE}_{i}\\;\\right],\\qquad\\mathrm{CE}_{i}=-\\log p_{\\theta}\\!\\left(x_{0}^{i}\\mid\\tilde{x}\\right),$$  |  | (3)\nL115: with both terms normalized per position. The first term is the standard masked loss over holes; the second teaches copy-else-fix at context positions. We use $\\lambda=0.25$ with uniform position weights, set once and not tuned; the defaults worked on the first attempt, and sweeps over $\\lambda$ and substituted-position upweighting are deferred (Section cite32†5 ).\nL116: Equation (cite57†3 ) is a deliberate surrogate rather than the exact variational bound of the composed process: principled ELBOs exist for mixed and interpolating kernels (cite42†Austin et al., 2021 ; cite43†Campbell et al., 2022 ; cite50†von Rütte et al., 2025 ), and their per-position weighting for the mixing term differs from ours; since $\\eta$ is small and the target is editor behavior rather than held-out likelihood, the principled weighting is likewise deferred.\nL117: We use no auxiliary input flag marking $K$ as potentially corrupted: content-only detection is the more general capability, and hole geometry is already visible through [MASK].\nL118: ### 3.4 Inference: reverse-time re-noising of the conditioning\nL119: \nL120: Because the finetuned model emits a meaningful distribution at every position, the observed-token probability\nL121: \nL122:  | $$s_{i}\\;=\\;p_{\\theta}\\!\\left(x_{0}^{i}=\\tilde{x}^{i}\\mid\\cdot\\right),\\qquad i\\in K,$$  |  | (4)\nL123: is a suspicion score for context.\nL124: The sampler wraps the standard reverse process (Algorithm cite58†1 ) with a corrector-like step that acts on the conditioning rather than the sample: while $t>t_{\\mathrm{lock}}=0.5$, context tokens with $s_{i}$ below a threshold annealed from $\\tau=0.5$ to $0.1$ (a time-dependent re-noising intensity) are returned to the absorbing state and join $H$, subject to an edit budget $B=\\lceil\\hat{\\rho}\\,|K|\\rceil$ with assumed corruption rate $\\hat{\\rho}=0.15$; the ordinary reverse dynamics then refill them with full bidirectional conditioning and iterative refinement.\nL125: Past $t_{\\mathrm{lock}}$ the context dynamics freeze. We use 64 reverse steps with greedy commits (with $R$ steps remaining and $m$ masked positions, the $\\lceil m/R\\rceil$ most confident predictions commit per step). One bookkeeping detail matters more than it looks: context tokens must enter the low-confidence commitment rule of cite56†Chang et al. (2022) ; cite48†Nie et al. (2025) with confidence $s_{i}$, not the indicator value 1, or the sampler never revisits them.\nL126: A cheap comparator keeps context frozen throughout and applies a single read-out repair at $t=0$, overwriting positions with $s_{i}<0.1$. The re-noising step is a heuristic: it is motivated by predictor–corrector samplers, but we do not derive it from, or show that it preserves, the reverse-time dynamics of the composed forward process.\nL127: Algorithm 1: annealed detect–remask–refill  r $\\leftarrow$ canvas: $H$ fully masked, $K$ = observed context\nL128: for t = 1 $\\to$ 0 in N steps:\nL129:    predict $p_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid r)$ at all positions\nL130:    if t > t_lock: # annealed re-noising of context\nL131:      for i in K, not yet edited, while edits < B:\nL132:        if $s_{i}<\\tau(t)$: r_{i} $\\leftarrow$ [MASK]; move i into H\nL133:    commit / remask hole tokens by low-confidence rule\nL134:    (context tokens carry confidence $s_{i}$, not 1)\nL135: Figure 1: The sampler. Context tokens may be re-noised into the absorbing state only early in the reverse trajectory; repaired positions are refilled by the standard reverse dynamics.\nL136: ## 4 Experiments\nL137: \nL138: ### 4.1 Setup\nL139: #### Model and training.\nL140: We finetune the public 170M-parameter MDLM checkpoint pretrained on OpenWebText (cite45†Sahoo et al., 2024 ; cite59†Gokaslan and Cohen, 2019 ), GPT-2 tokenizer (cite60†Radford et al., 2019 ), on FineWeb-Edu (cite61†Penedo et al., 2024 ) packed into 512-token blocks (400k training blocks, $\\approx$205M tokens; evaluation blocks held out from documents 400k positions downstream).\nL141: Every arm trains for 8,000 steps at effective batch $32\\times 512$ ($\\approx$131M tokens seen), learning rate $3{\\times}10^{-5}$ cosine, bf16, identical seeds. The budget is deliberately modest: under 4.5 h per arm on a 6 GB RTX 3060 laptop GPU (21 min on an RTX 5090).\nL142: Training dynamics show the intended division of labor: clean-context cross-entropy falls from 3.93 to 0.03 nats (learned trust), substituted-context cross-entropy from 11.2 to 2.5 (learned repair), while hole cross-entropy tracks the vanilla arm (infilling intact).\nL143: #### Evaluation protocol.\nL144: Thirteen evaluation cells shared by every arm in a fully paired design (identical hole spans and corruption positions): planted context-corruption rate $\\rho\\in\\{0,0.02,0.05,0.1,0.2\\}$ crossed with $\\{$uniform, plausible$\\}$ corruptions at 30% hole coverage, plus coverage $\\{10\\%,50\\%\\}$ at $\\rho=0.1$; 256 passages per cell.\nL145: Plausible evaluation corruptions are GPT-2 samples conditioned on left context (top-50, true token excluded), a generator family disjoint from every training noise family; plausible-corruption results are therefore out-of-distribution for every arm. Ground truth is the original passage.\nL146: #### Arms.\nL147: \nL148: Table cite62†1 lists the nine arms. Arms sharing weights differ only in the sampler, isolating training-time from inference-time contributions; EXT is an external-detector baseline that flags the top-$k$ most surprising context tokens under GPT-2 autoregressive surprisal ($k$ matched per sequence to DP’s edit count, which is deliberately generous to the baseline) and refills them with the vanilla arm-A model.\nL149: Table 1: Experimental arms. C/D share weights, as do CP/DP.\nL150: Arm  | Weights  | Inference  | Isolates\nL151: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL152: base  | pretrained  | frozen context  | pre-finetune reference\nL153: A  | vanilla SFT ($\\eta=0$)  | frozen context  | baseline\nL154: B  | vanilla SFT ($\\eta=0$)  | trust-annealed  | inference-only contribution\nL155: C  | $\\eta$-uniform  | frozen $+$ read-out repair  | training contribution alone\nL156: D  | $\\eta$-uniform  | trust-annealed  | full method, uniform kernel\nL157: E  | $\\eta=0.15$ fixed, no mixture  | trust-annealed  | value of the $\\eta{=}0$ mixture\nL158: CP  | $\\eta$-hard-negative mixture  | frozen $+$ read-out repair  | data-dependent kernel, cheap inference\nL159: DP  | $\\eta$-hard-negative mixture  | trust-annealed  | full method, data-dependent kernel\nL160: EXT  | vanilla SFT (arm-A refill)  | GPT-2 surprisal flags  | external-detector baseline\nL161: #### Metrics.\nL162: Repair: detection precision/recall over planted positions, correction accuracy (edited to the ground-truth token), and the false-edit rate on clean tokens, our primary safety metric. Infill: token-F1 against ground truth, computed per hole span and averaged over all spans in a cell; deltas are reported in points ($\\times 100$) with paired-bootstrap 95% confidence intervals (10k resamples of passages, same statistic as the tables).\nL163: Passage quality: mean NLL of the completed passage under a gpt2-large judge (cite60†Radford et al., 2019 ), since exact match cannot credit valid-but-different repairs. Containment: infill token accuracy binned by distance to the nearest corrupted context token. Non-regression: clean-context ($\\rho=0$) metrics and the held-out variational bound relative to arm A.\nL164: ### 4.2 Results\nL165: Table 2: Headline cell: plausible corruptions, $\\rho=0.1$, 30% hole coverage ($n=256$, paired). Paired-bootstrap F1 deltas vs. A (points): DP $+0.51$ $[-0.26,+1.28]$; D $+0.38$ $[-0.36,+1.09]$; E $+0.51$ $[-0.30,+1.31]$; CP $+0.40$ $[-0.31,+1.10]$; none is significant.\nL166: Arm  | Infill F1  | Det. P  | Det. R  | False-edit %  | Correction  | Judge NLL $\\downarrow$\nL167: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL168: base  | 0.275  | –  | –  | –  | –  | 3.250\nL169: A  | 0.287  | –  | –  | –  | –  | 3.237\nL170: B  | 0.286  | 0.572  | 0.035  | 0.29  | 0.756  | 3.209\nL171: C  | 0.286  | 0.716  | 0.058  | 0.26  | 0.756  | 3.173\nL172: D  | 0.291  | 0.668  | 0.150  | 0.83  | 0.730  | 3.083\nL173: E  | 0.292  | 0.625  | 0.272  | 1.82  | 0.716  | 2.980\nL174: CP  | 0.291  | 0.878  | 0.197  | 0.31  | 0.773  | 3.052\nL175: DP  | 0.292  | 0.752  | 0.350  | 1.28  | 0.743  | 2.902\nL176: EXT  | 0.279  | 0.062  | 0.029  | 4.87  | 0.260  | 3.101\nL177: #### Repair (H1).\nL178: The training-time forward process appears necessary for the capability: the vanilla model with the same sampler (arm B) detects almost nothing (recall 0.035), because a model whose training dynamics never visit a wrong unmasked state assigns no meaningful likelihood to observed positions; the pretrained checkpoint gives $\\approx$50% probability to uniform-random garbage in context.\nL179: After the $\\eta$-finetune, conspicuous corruptions are caught nearly completely: at uniform $\\rho=0.1$, arm D reaches recall 0.959 $[0.954,0.963]$ at precision 0.909 with a 1.07% false-edit rate (DP: recall 0.959 at precision 0.853, 1.84% false-edit), and corrections land on the exact ground-truth token 52–77% of the time across $\\eta$-arms and corruption types.\nL180: On out-of-distribution plausible corruptions (Table cite63†2 ), the data-dependent kernel more than doubles recall over the uniform kernel (DP 0.350 vs. D 0.150) at precision 0.752, though with a higher false-edit rate (1.28% vs. 0.83%), consistent with training noise nearer the data conditional transferring better to corruptions drawn near it. Even so, two thirds of plausible corruptions go undetected. False-edit rates on fully clean context stay between 0.07% and 0.39% for the mixture-trained arms.\nL181: At $\\rho=0.2$ recall pins at $\\approx$0.76 across $\\eta$-arms; this is the $\\hat{\\rho}=0.15$ edit budget binding, not a detection ceiling.\nL182: #### Containment (H2).\nL183: Where corruption actually poisons infilling, the method removes the poison. Under uniform corruption at $\\rho=0.1$, vanilla arms show the contamination signature: infill accuracy drops to 0.088–0.11 in the 1–2-token bin adjacent to a corruption, below their own 0.14–0.16 at distance 3–4.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRank Measures of Fine-Tuning UpdatesNeed a Noise Baseline (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22438v1)\nciteturn41255view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22438v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 624\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProgressive Misalignment Can Emerge from Benign Recursive Self-Distillation (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22467v1)\nciteturn41255view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22467v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 448\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPredicting Privileged Information Effectiveness for On-Policy Self-Distillation (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22512v1)\nciteturn41255view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22512v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1041\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDon’t Retrain, Remix: Adaptation of Web Agents using Mixture Hypernetworks (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22539v1)\nciteturn41255view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22539v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 600\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn41255view5 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStructured Authority Injection: Persistent Human Control for LLM Agents via Typed MCP Overrides (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22373v1)\nciteturn41255view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22373v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 298\n"
